WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.811
Back to the move.

2
00:00:03.811 --> 00:00:10.021
A helper with a clipboard reads each box's label and writes new cards, which travel with the move.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:19.999
This time, the box with the @Builder note gets a builder: LampBuilder, a small class that collects a lamp's values one call at a time, then builds the lamp.

4
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.275
Here's a question many of us are too shy to ask.

5
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:25.238
A generated file is just text.

6
00:00:25.238 --> 00:00:28.445
So why would anyone need a library to print text?

7
00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.997
Lesson 2's string template worked fine.

8
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.211
Let's try it.

9
00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:39.534
The template pastes each name and each type into a line of text.

10
00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:41.628
For Lamp, the builder compiles.

11
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:42.436
Now Load.

12
00:00:42.436 --> 00:00:48.087
It lives in package movemate.van, and its field is a Box, from package movemate.

13
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:51.548
What happens when the build compiles its builder?

14
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.408
Unresolved reference Box.

15
00:00:56.408 --> 00:00:59.011
The template never wrote the import.

16
00:00:59.011 --> 00:01:00.891
And notice who complained.

17
00:01:00.891 --> 00:01:06.821
Not KSP, which wrote the file without a word, but the Kotlin compiler, afterwards.

18
00:01:06.821 --> 00:01:10.292
A generated file is compiled exactly like yours.

19
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.128
The others slip the same way.

20
00:01:13.128 --> 00:01:15.853
Lamp's shade gets a second question mark.

21
00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:18.910
A nested Drawer must be called Kitchen.Drawer.

22
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.832
And a property named in, a Kotlin keyword, needs backticks.

23
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.450
In the moving house, that's handwriting.

24
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.553
It smudges a mark.

25
00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.984
It abbreviates without saying what the short name means.

26
00:01:29.984 --> 00:01:32.066
It forgets which room a box is in.

27
00:01:32.066 --> 00:01:35.129
And it writes a word the reader takes as an order.

28
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:37.230
You could patch each slip by hand.

29
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.310
Keep patching, and one day you notice you've written a small program that prints Kotlin.

30
00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.871
The good news: somebody already wrote it, and tested it far more than we would.

31
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.515
So swap the handwriting for a label printer.

32
00:01:50.515 --> 00:01:51.934
You don't type letters.

33
00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:57.303
You hand it the parts: a class called LampBuilder, a field called name, of type String.

34
00:01:57.303 --> 00:02:05.448
It prints them, and it knows the rules: which short names need an import, where the question marks go, and which words are keywords.

35
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:06.456
Let's map it.

36
00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:11.786
The printer is KotlinPoet, a library from Square that writes Kotlin source files.

37
00:02:11.786 --> 00:02:18.960
Each part is a spec: FileSpec for a file, TypeSpec for a class, then PropertySpec, FunSpec and ParameterSpec.

38
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.843
You make each one with a builder, and finish it with build.

39
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:29.487
Let's shrink it to one spec: a function called greet, which returns a String, Hello from Lamp.

40
00:02:29.487 --> 00:02:31.875
Before it prints, guess what comes out.

41
00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.031
One line, with three habits.

42
00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:38.792
It wrote public by itself.

43
00:02:38.792 --> 00:02:42.316
It turned a one line return into an expression body.

44
00:02:42.316 --> 00:02:44.959
And alone, it spells out kotlin.String.

45
00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:48.415
Inside a file, that's plain String, plus an import.

46
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.573
Public on everything is like a mover who writes this way up on every box, even the round ones.

47
00:02:54.573 --> 00:02:58.704
But it's on purpose: explicit visibility keeps the code portable.

48
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:01.449
Java has an older sibling, JavaPoet.

49
00:03:01.449 --> 00:03:08.693
A MethodSpec, a dollar sign where KotlinPoet has a percent sign, and the same greet as a Java method.

50
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:16.328
That string, return %S, is a format string: code text with blanks, filled from the arguments after it.

51
00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:20.088
Four blanks, called placeholders, do nearly all the work.

52
00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:21.328
%T takes a type.

53
00:03:21.328 --> 00:03:26.040
It prints the type's name, and puts the type on the file's import list.

54
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.187
%N takes a name, and adds backticks when the name needs them.

55
00:03:30.187 --> 00:03:32.997
Give it in, and out comes in, in backticks.

56
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:39.796
%S takes a string, and writes a string literal: in quotes, with quotes and dollar signs escaped.

57
00:03:39.933 --> 00:03:43.993
And %L pastes a value as it is, no questions asked.

58
00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:47.890
That %S line looks like a cat walked across the keyboard.

59
00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:53.493
But it's the same text, dollar sign and all, written so Kotlin reads it back exactly.

60
00:03:53.493 --> 00:04:00.281
Each placeholder knows the one rule its kind of value needs, which is just what the handwriting forgot.

61
00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:03.454
So pass types with %T, and names with %N.

62
00:04:03.454 --> 00:04:06.368
Never paste them into the format string.

63
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:10.085
Now the whole builder, spec by spec.

64
00:04:10.085 --> 00:04:16.037
Watch the top of the file: every type that goes in through %T joins the import list.

65
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.636
FileSpec starts the file, with its package.

66
00:04:19.636 --> 00:04:21.331
TypeSpec adds the class.

67
00:04:21.331 --> 00:04:28.322
The first PropertySpec adds name, a String that starts out empty, and import kotlin.String appears.

68
00:04:28.322 --> 00:04:33.265
bulbs is an Int, so import kotlin.Int joins it, in alphabetical order.

69
00:04:33.265 --> 00:04:36.089
shade is a String again, so nothing new.

70
00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:38.961
And shade has one question mark, not two.

71
00:04:38.961 --> 00:04:45.731
The type is made nullable with copy, nullable equals true, which leaves an already nullable type alone.

72
00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:51.525
Then a FunSpec per setter: each fills its slot and hands the builder back.

73
00:04:51.525 --> 00:04:53.515
A last FunSpec writes build.

74
00:04:53.515 --> 00:04:59.129
And writeTo hands the file to KSP, together with the box it came from, Lamp.kt.

75
00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:03.359
Now the one that failed: Load, in package movemate.van.

76
00:05:03.359 --> 00:05:08.046
Same specs, and the top of the file reads: import movemate.Box.

77
00:05:08.046 --> 00:05:10.426
The line the handwriting forgot.

78
00:05:10.426 --> 00:05:12.584
Nobody typed a single import.

79
00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:15.482
Here the picture breaks.

80
00:05:15.482 --> 00:05:18.512
A label printer prints exactly what you type.

81
00:05:18.512 --> 00:05:23.561
KotlinPoet edits as it prints: imports, public, backticks, a reshaped body.

82
00:05:23.561 --> 00:05:26.523
So read its output once before you trust it.

83
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.020
And it has a blind spot.

84
00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:35.835
It describes declarations, not what happens inside a body: the body of build is just a format string.

85
00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:41.436
Fill name from the bulbs slot, and KotlinPoet prints that mistake, neatly indented.

86
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.715
It's a printer, not a proofreader.

87
00:05:43.715 --> 00:05:50.061
The compiler reads for meaning, so every test in the lab compiles the generated builder, and calls it.

88
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.341
So, why a library to print text?

89
00:05:53.341 --> 00:05:57.959
Because generated code is ordinary source code, with a strict reader.

90
00:05:57.959 --> 00:06:05.521
Describe it as specs, pass types with %T and names with %N, and the printer writes the imports and the backticks.

91
00:06:05.521 --> 00:06:07.461
Then compile it, and call it.

92
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.373
Quick check.

93
00:06:08.373 --> 00:06:10.627
Crate has a property called object.

94
00:06:10.627 --> 00:06:14.169
Which placeholder carries its name, and what comes out?

95
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:17.642
%N.

96
00:06:17.642 --> 00:06:23.216
It prints object in backticks, because object is a keyword in Kotlin.
